WEBVTT

NOTE Extended descriptions: pause at cue start until speech completes.

1
00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:04.600
The opening title reads: Teaching Channel, DIY Certified. Putting the camera in teachers' hands.

2
00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:13.700
Genevieve speaks to the camera from a classroom. An on-screen note recommends the earlier video, Project LIT Bronx: Shifting the Culture of Reading, on Tch dot org.

3
00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:37.700
Photos show boxes and shelves of multiple copies of the same books, including Ghost Boys, Long Way Down, and All American Boys.

4
00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:49.600
An example Project LIT Nashville calendar pairs each meeting's date, school, time, and book cover. A flyer for All American Boys gives the time, three to four, and Room 505, inviting readers for discussion, trivia, snacks, and prizes.

5
00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:52.900
A Project LIT Bronx flyer pairs Long Way Down by Jason Reynolds with January 25, and The Hate U Give by Angie Thomas with March 1. Both meetings are three to four p.m.

6
00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:07.700
Students pose together in a classroom, each holding a copy of The Hate U Give with its cover facing the camera.

7
00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:14.900
A spreadsheet has trivia questions in the left column and their answers on the right. Examples from The Hate U Give ask the name of Starr's boyfriend, answered Chris, and Starr and Chris's favorite TV show, answered The Fresh Prince of Bel Air.

8
00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:33.500
A colorful Project LIT meeting flyer invites everyone to help plan the next book club, prepare for a Nashville trip, and make a Project LIT video. It lists Thursday, April 12, two forty-five to three thirty, Room 411.

9
00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:40.600
The All American Boys flyer appears again. Beneath a silhouette with raised hands is the hashtag, Rashad is absent again today.

10
00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:41.600
Beside a laptop, a woman speaks into a corded telephone handset to make the school announcement.

11
00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:23.100
Photos show students holding books and posing beside a handmade red Project LIT poster.

12
00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:31.600
Beside Dear Martin by Nic Stone, a printed agenda for the May 23, 2018 Bronx Studio School book club divides the hour into a welcome, small-group discussion, whole-group discussion, trivia, and a group photo.

13
00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:38.600
The Dear Martin discussion guide provides lines for group members' names, then asks the group to choose a facilitator and review discussion norms. Questions occupy the left column, with blank space for discussion notes on the right. Participants may take notes, but are told to focus on discussion rather than answering every question.

14
00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:47.100
The guide asks which scenes stood out and why, which characters readers related to, and why students and adults should discuss Dear Martin. Further prompts ask whether the book reflects society, how it compares with expectations, what readers would ask the author, and what actions they are inspired to take. One prompt on this Dear Martin sheet still names The Crossover.

15
00:02:59.500 --> 00:02:59.600
A photo shows participants seated around a small table with discussion sheets and snacks. The next classroom view shows several groups seated at clusters of desks.

16
00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:09.600
At the front of the classroom, student facilitators stand facing the seated group.

17
00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:23.600
A close view shows teammates leaning over the same answer sheet as one writes. In the wider classroom view, participants work together at clusters of desks.

18
00:03:56.500 --> 00:03:56.600
For the group photo, participants stand and crouch together in a school hallway, smiling and holding their books with the covers facing the camera.

19
00:03:58.000 --> 00:03:58.100
Genevieve returns on camera for the closing remarks.
